{
  "dom_id": "step-q47814",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The graph of the function $f(x)$ passes through $(1,4)$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=-3.$ Find $L(x)$, the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the graph of $f(x)$ passes through $(1,4)$, we have that $f(1)=4.$ Therefore, the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =4-3(x-1) \\\\  & =4-3x+3 \\\\  & =-3x+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
